# Role attestations

Download this `.livemd` file, open it in [Livebook](https://livebook.dev), and choose
**Open → Import from file**, then **Run all**. Use Elixir 1.18 or later and OTP 27 or later
with access to public Hex. No secrets or external services are needed.

Bind a subject public key to an issuer role. Verify its attestor window and subject binding,
then refuse self-attestation, expiry, an overlong window, and cross-profile bytes.

The host chooses the attestor it trusts and the role it requires. Attestation facts report
`trust: :not_evaluated`; they do not grant authority or reserve replay. Keys stay in memory,
and this notebook starts no processes and creates no files.

[Open this 1.1.0 release notebook](https://livebook.dev/run?url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fbaselabs%2Fbounded_authority_protocol%2Fblob%2Fv1.1.0%2Fdocs%2Fnotebooks%2Frole-attestations.livemd).
The launch opens this journey at its immutable release tag.

## Setup

Install the exact published package. Fixed evaluation times keep the results reproducible;
only safe semantic values are persisted, never keys, signatures, tokens, or nonces.

```elixir
Mix.install([{:bounded_authority_protocol, "== 1.1.0"}])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:ok
```

## Generate attestor and subject keys

Generate distinct Ed25519 keys for the attestor and subject. A role binding needs no grant
or request proof.

```elixir
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.RoleAttestation.V1, as: RoleAttestationV1
alias BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.HistoricalPublicKey

{attestor_public, attestor_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)
{subject_public, _subject_private} = :crypto.generate_key(:eddsa, :ed25519)

now = 1_800_001_000
:keys_ready
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:keys_ready
```

## Build and sign the attestation

The attestor signs a standalone `ba+role-attestation` JWS. Its payload names the subject key,
role, identifier, and validity window.

```elixir
attestation = %RoleAttestationV1.RoleAttestation{
  attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
  jti: "urn:example:attestation:2026-001",
  key_id: "issuer-key-1",
  public_key: subject_public,
  role: "issuer",
  nbf: now,
  exp: now + 3_600
}

{:ok, attestation_input} = RoleAttestationV1.attestation_signing_input(attestation, %{})

attestation_signature =
  :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, attestation_input.message, [attestor_private, :ed25519])

{:ok, attestation_compact} =
  RoleAttestationV1.assemble_compact(attestation_input, attestation_signature)

:attestation_signed
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
:attestation_signed
```

## Verify the subject binding and window

Supply the expected subject independently and an already-trusted historical attestor key.
The attestation window must fit entirely inside that key window.

```elixir
expected_attestation = %RoleAttestationV1.ExpectedAttestation{
  attestor: %HistoricalPublicKey{
    key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
    public_key: attestor_public,
    valid_from: now - 600,
    valid_before: now + 7_200
  },
  subject_key_id: "issuer-key-1",
  subject_public_key: subject_public,
  now: now + 60,
  bounds: %{}
}

{:ok, attestation_facts} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, expected_attestation)

Map.take(attestation_facts, [:attestor_key_id, :subject_key_id, :role, :trust])
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
%{attestor_key_id: "authority-attestor-1", subject_key_id: "issuer-key-1", role: "issuer", trust: :not_evaluated}
```

## Refuse a true self-attestation

Sign a binding to the attestor itself and make the expected subject match. The refusal then
comes from the structural self-attestation rule, rather than a subject mismatch.

```elixir
# A true self-attestation: the payload binds the attestor's own key id and key material, and
# the expected subject matches it, so every binding check passes and only the structural
# self-attestation rejection can fire.
self_attestation = %{attestation | key_id: "authority-attestor-1", public_key: attestor_public}

{:ok, self_input} = RoleAttestationV1.attestation_signing_input(self_attestation, %{})
self_signature = :crypto.sign(:eddsa, :none, self_input.message, [attestor_private, :ed25519])
{:ok, self_compact} = RoleAttestationV1.assemble_compact(self_input, self_signature)

{:error, :invalid} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(self_compact, %{
    expected_attestation
    | subject_key_id: "authority-attestor-1",
      subject_public_key: attestor_public
  })
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
{:error, :invalid}
```

## Refuse expired or outliving attestations

The acceptance interval is half-open: evaluation at `exp` fails. An otherwise current
attestation also fails when it outlives its attestor key.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
    expected_attestation
    | now: now + 3_600
  })

{:error, :invalid} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
    expected_attestation
    | attestor: %{expected_attestation.attestor | valid_before: now + 1_800}
  })

[:expired, :outliving_attestor]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:expired, :outliving_attestor]
```

## Refuse a different subject

A signature from a trusted attestor does not permit substituting a subject. The expected key id
and public key both bind the statement to the subject the host intended.

```elixir
{:error, :invalid} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
    expected_attestation
    | subject_key_id: "other-subject"
  })

{:error, :invalid} =
  RoleAttestationV1.verify_attestation(attestation_compact, %{
    expected_attestation
    | subject_public_key: attestor_public
  })

[:subject_key_id, :subject_public_key]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:subject_key_id, :subject_public_key]
```

## Refuse cross-profile decoding

These bytes decode as a role attestation but never as a grant or content assertion. Selecting
a profile remains an explicit host choice.

```elixir
{:ok, _decoded} = RoleAttestationV1.decode_attestation(attestation_compact, %{})
{:error, :invalid} = BoundedAuthorityProtocol.V1.decode_grant(attestation_compact, %{})

{:error, :invalid} =
  BoundedAuthorityProtocol.ContentAssertion.V1.decode_assertion(attestation_compact, %{})

[:grant_profile, :content_profile]
```

<!-- livebook:{"output":true} -->

```
[:grant_profile, :content_profile]
```

See [trusted keys and rotation](../guides/trusted-keys-and-rotation.md) for trust installation
and the [role-attestation specification](../../spec/bap-role-attestation-v1.md) for the profile.
